RULE NUMBER
008

TITLE
Forge: Generate the kind-root e2e/manifest.yaml

ROLE
You are the Planton Code Partner working inside the developer’s local workspace in Cursor. This rule authors the kind's base test manifest at `catalog/<provider>/<kindfolder>/e2e/manifest.yaml` -- a complete, protovalidate-valid example manifest for the kind.

THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING TWICE
- **It is the kind's published Example.** The reference generator
  (`make generate-reference`, `pkg/explain/refgen`) reads `e2e/manifest.yaml`, round-trips
  it into the typed message, runs protovalidate on it, and embeds it as the Example block
  of the kind's `reference.md` page. A broken manifest fails the regeneration; a missing
  one renders the page without an Example.
- **It is the E2E testability marker AND the deployed fixture.** The E2E framework's
  discovery (`e2e/framework/discovery/`) treats the PRESENCE of `e2e/manifest.yaml` as
  "this kind is testable", and the harness deploys EXACTLY this manifest. What you
  write here is what runs against the real provider on both engines.

Author it accordingly: a realistic, safe-defaults manifest that both teaches a reader and
deploys cleanly -- not a throwaway scratch file.

VARIANTS
Variant manifests may sit beside the base manifest as `manifest-<variant>.yaml`
(e.g. `catalog/aws/awskinesisfirehose/e2e/manifest-iceberg.yaml`) when one example cannot
carry every meaningfully different shape. Only `manifest.yaml` is the testability marker
and the embedded Example; variants are additional fixtures.

TOOLS
- Proto Readers: reuse `_rules/catalog-kind/_scripts/spec_proto_reader.py` and `_rules/catalog-kind/_scripts/api_reader.py`.
- Writer: `_rules/catalog-kind/_scripts/e2e_manifest_write.py` writes the manifest deterministically (creating `e2e/` if needed).
- Reference kinds (live ground truth): `catalog/aws/awsvpc/e2e/manifest.yaml`
  and `catalog/aws/awsalb/e2e/manifest.yaml` -- canonical base test manifests.

SCOPE
- Target: `catalog/<provider>/<kindfolder>/e2e/manifest.yaml`
- Create/update a complete, valid manifest with safe defaults.

SEQUENTIAL STEPS
1) Read: Use proto readers to obtain `<Kind>`, `api_version`, and essential fields from `<Kind>Spec`.
2) Draft: Generate the manifest YAML modeled on the reference kind `manifest.yaml` files.
3) Write: `cat <<'YAML' | python3 _rules/catalog-kind/_scripts/e2e_manifest_write.py --provider <provider> --kindfolder <kindfolder> --stdin | cat`
   - If edits are needed, refine and re-run (≤3).

STRINGVALUEORREF FIELDS (do not use bare scalars)
Every `StringValueOrRef` field in the spec MUST use the structured wrapper form in the
manifest -- `value: <literal>` (or `valueFrom: {...}` for references):

```yaml
spec:
  resourceGroup:
    value: test-rg
```

A bare scalar (`resourceGroup: test-rg`) fails manifest validation at plan time with a
cryptic protoyaml syntax error (`proto: syntax error ... unexpected token`), and nothing
catches it earlier -- the YAML is well-formed and only the proto unmarshal rejects it.
Identify the `StringValueOrRef` fields by reading `spec.proto` before drafting.

EXERCISE CONDITIONAL ENUM SEAMS (a plan only proves the fields it carries)
The plan-time validation below can only catch translation bugs in fields the manifest
actually sets. Enum fields behind a conditional mapping (a `locals.tf` ternary or
lookup map) are the highest-risk seam: a map keyed on the wrong string silently
resolves null and the feature is dropped with a green plan. When the spec carries
enums whose value names are prefixed for in-file disambiguation (per-OS or per-mode
vocabularies), include at least one such field in the manifest so the plan proves
the mapping end to end.

PLACEHOLDER ARM IDS MUST USE AZURERM'S CANONICAL SEGMENT CASING
When a field carries an Azure resource ID (a literal placeholder in the manifest),
copy the resource-type path segment's casing from azurerm's own ID parser -- it
validates segments CASE-SENSITIVELY at plan time. The trap: ARM documents some
provider types in PascalCase while the ID segment azurerm accepts is camelCase
(e.g. the type `Microsoft.Network/ApplicationGatewayWebApplicationFirewallPolicies`
parses only as `.../providers/Microsoft.Network/applicationGatewayWebApplicationFirewallPolicies/<name>`).
A wrong-case placeholder fails the plan with "the segment at position N didn't
match". Find the canonical form in the provider clone's `_resource.go` ID
validation or its acceptance-test fixtures -- never from the ARM type name.

REBUILD THE CLI AFTER PROTO CHANGES (stale-CLI failure class)
`planton tofu plan` / `planton validate-manifest` parse the manifest with the
protos compiled INTO the installed CLI binary, not the working tree's. After
adding spec fields, rebuild and reinstall before validating
(`go build -o ~/bin/planton .` from the repo root); an
`unknown field "<newField>"` rejection on a manifest you know is correct means
a stale CLI, not a bad manifest.

SOME PROVIDER RESOURCES READ THE CLOUD AT PLAN TIME (offline-plan boundary)
A few resources phone home during plan: their `CustomizeDiff` fetches a
referenced resource to validate pairings (e.g. `azurerm_linux_function_app`
reads the referenced Service Plan to check its tier), which no synthetic
placeholder ID can satisfy offline. When the plan fails ONLY on such a live
read (the error names the referenced resource, not your HCL), the offline
proof for that kind is `tofu validate` plus a plan that stops at the read --
record the boundary in the kind's GUIDE.md, and let the live E2E chain
(real referenced resource) carry the full plan/apply proof.

SUCCESS CRITERIA
- Manifest exists at `catalog/<provider>/<kindfolder>/e2e/manifest.yaml`.
- Complete, valid spec with safe defaults; no credentials.
- The manifest passes protovalidate (`planton validate-manifest <path>` with a CLI built
  from the working tree) -- the reference generator will reject it otherwise.
- Every `StringValueOrRef` field uses the `value:`/`valueFrom:` wrapper form.
- Conditionally-mapped enum fields (especially prefix-disambiguated values) are
  represented so the plan exercises their `locals.tf` translation.
- The manifest survives `planton tofu plan --manifest <path> --module-dir <abs path to .../iac/tf>`
  (the plan run is the validation step that catches wrapper-form mistakes).

OUTPUT STYLE IN CHAT
- Show created path and a short snippet (no secrets).
